  "text_input": "Patient is a 78-year-old female who presented with a dry, non-productive cough for the past 3 months, which worsened over the last week. The cough was initially intermittent and mild, occurring mainly in the evening, but has become more frequent and persistent, associated with sleep disturbance and throat irritation, increasing when lying down. She has a known history of Decompensated Chronic Liver Parenchymal Disease (DCLD) secondary to Hepatitis B, portal hypertension, and grade 3 esophageal varices. Physical examination revealed bilateral crepitations and distended flanks with shifting dullness. Investigations confirmed bilateral lobar pneumonia and anemia of chronic disease. Echo showed concentric LVH and an EF of 55%.",
